class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // If n is less than 1, then it cannot be a power of three
        if (n < 1) {
            return false;
        }

        // Use a while loop to repeatedly divide n by 3 until n becomes 1 or smaller than 3
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }

        // If n is equal to 1, then it is a power of three
        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

This algorithm first checks if `n` is less than 1, in which case it returns `false`. Then it uses a while loop to repeatedly divide `n` by 3 as long as `n` is divisible by 3. After the loop ends, `n` will either be 1 (in which case it is a power of three and we return `true`) or it will be between 1 and 3 (in which case it is not a power of three and we return `false`). This algorithm has a time complexity of O(log3(n)) since the number of iterations is proportional to the number of bits needed to represent `n` in base 3.

